subsection:: Discrete nonlinear iteration and excitability

link::Classes/HenonMap:: uses a quadratic recurrence, link::Classes/GbmanMap:: an absolute-value recurrence, and link::Classes/LatoocarfianMap:: a documented two-sine recurrence. link::Classes/IkedaMap:: models nonlinear optical rotation; link::Classes/LoziMap:: introduces a piecewise-linear fold; link::Classes/TinkerbellMap:: uses a polynomial coordinate update. link::Classes/CliffordMap:: and link::Classes/DeJongMap:: use different trigonometric recurrences. link::Classes/CircleMap:: is a wrapped phase recurrence that can show locking. These raw maps follow deterministic rules and can be used for irregular sequencing or coordinate modulation; parameters can also produce periodic motion or divergence.

link::Classes/CoupledLogisticMap:: exchanges influence between two recurrence states. link::Classes/CoupledMapLattice:: adds a spatial ring with local coupling, allowing collective patterns to emerge from interaction. Neither coupling nor a chaotic-looking trace implies a source of random innovations.

link::Classes/RulkovMap:: belongs technically to discrete maps but behaviorally to excitability: fast and slow states can produce quiescence, spikes and bursts. That role connects it to the continuous voltage/recovery model link::Classes/Izhikevich::, whose threshold and reset generate discrete spikes. They should not be treated simply as two generic chaotic oscillators.

subsection:: Continuous feedback, history and populations

link::Classes/MackeyGlass:: stores delayed history, so the derivative depends on an earlier value as well as the present. This is explicit deterministic temporal memory. link::Classes/Chua:: instead exposes the three instantaneous states of a nonlinear circuit; continuous feedback can organize low-dimensional chaos without a delay line.

link::Classes/WilsonCowan:: remembers excitation and inhibition at the population level. Mutual feedback and separate time constants can yield stable activity or oscillations. Its simplified logistic equations contain no random source: use a separate stochastic UGen when perturbations are wanted. link::Classes/Lorenz96:: extends continuous feedback across a cyclic lattice. Local nonlinear interaction, damping and forcing organize a spatial population, not independent cell-wise noise.

link::Classes/KuramotoNetwork:: couples phases through a collective mean field. Different natural frequencies can coexist with partial or strong synchronization; the order parameter measures coordination without a master clock. With noise=0 it is a deterministic phase flow; positive noise adds external-model phase diffusion. It is conceptually distinct from a fixed-frequency oscillator bank and from unbounded Lorenz coordinates.

subsection:: Independent shaped randomness and geometry

Triggered distributions are memoryless samplers for fixed parameters: a trigger draws a new value, then the output holds. Their RNG state and sample-and-hold output do not encode evolving process history. Changing a parameter from another process can of course make the resulting patch dependent in time.

link::Classes/TGammaRand:: and link::Classes/TWeibullRand:: describe positive quantities with tunable shape; link::Classes/TLogNormalRand:: models positive multiplicative variation. They are useful for durations, gains and skewed scales, with different near-zero and tail behavior. link::Classes/TPoissonRand:: gives event counts; link::Classes/TGeometricRand:: gives failures before the first success; link::Classes/TNegBinomialRand:: extends that to a specified success count. These are integer-valued count/failure models, not continuous curves.

link::Classes/TCauchyRand::, link::Classes/TStableRand:: and link::Classes/TLevyRand:: make extreme values more likely than Gaussian sampling in their heavy-tailed regimes. TStableRand includes a Gaussian endpoint; TLevyRand is one-sided. Accumulating symmetric stable increments over elapsed time produces link::Classes/LevyFlight::, a different object from a sequence of held independent values.

link::Classes/TTruncNormalRand:: samples a Gaussian conditioned to lie inside an interval. Clipping an ordinary Gaussian instead piles probability at the boundaries. link::Classes/TVonMisesRand:: describes probability on a circle: angles near minus pi and plus pi are neighbors, and concentration gathers directions without inventing a linear seam.

link::Classes/TDirichletRand:: describes probability on a simplex. Shares are nonnegative and sum to one, so allocating more to one component leaves less for others. Concentrations below one favor sparse allocations; large equal concentrations favor balance. This can redistribute a fixed amplitude or routing budget without making independent gains compete through an after-the-fact limiter.

subsection:: Temporal memory and stochastic boundaries

link::Classes/OUProcess:: adds a restoring drift to Gaussian diffusion. It has a stationary equilibrium for positive reversion but is mathematically unbounded, not a random walk clipped to a range. link::Classes/MaternNoise:: extends this Gaussian family with hidden derivatives and independently specified variance, correlation scale and smoothness. Its half-integer modes use exact finite-step linear-SDE transitions.

link::Classes/FractionalNoise:: approximates scale-dependent spectra with a finite band of OU components. link::Classes/FBrownianMotion:: approximates fractional motion with finite-memory filtered innovations, allowing persistent or antipersistent increments. These finite-band/finite-memory implementations do not provide unlimited scale-free memory. Matérn instead sets a finite correlation scale and local differentiability explicitly.

link::Classes/BoundedWalk:: puts a boundary rule inside the process: reflection, wrapping, clipping, bounded resampling and absorption create different subsequent histories. Applying an output clip to an unbounded model is a sonic mapping and does not change that model's internal motion.

subsection:: Event time, interaction and inhibition

link::Classes/RenewalTrig:: organizes time through independent inter-event intervals. Different interval laws change regularity and tail behavior without a metrical grid. link::Classes/HawkesTrig:: remembers past events as temporarily increased intensity, producing self-exciting bursts. link::Classes/MultiHawkesTrig:: lets one stream's events raise other streams' intensities, creating directional contagion and call-and-response. Causal influence between streams is different from mere covariance among simultaneous random values.

link::Classes/SelfCorrectingTrig:: builds event pressure during silence and reduces it after an event. This inhibitory feedback can resist clustering without becoming a metronome. link::Classes/ShotNoise:: instead remembers material response: marked arrivals contribute to an exponentially decaying sum. Sparse impacts remain distinct; overlapping impacts create a continuous texture. Arrival timing and the accumulated response are separate outputs.

subsection:: Discrete state, dwell time and learned preference

link::Classes/MarkovChain:: selects a transition row using the current state: the next choice depends on where the process is now. link::Classes/SemiMarkov:: separates destination choice from residence time through a state-specific duration distribution. This makes phrase lengths or texture dwell times explicit.

link::Classes/PolyaUrn:: carries a different history. Every chosen category increases its own future weight, so an early accident can become a lasting preference. Zero reinforcement removes this adaptation. Its entropy reports current diversity; individual runs can favor different categories even when the ensemble remains symmetric. Reloading its initial weights restarts preference, while changing a Markov row changes a transition rule.

link::Classes/MultiGaussNoise:: creates instantaneous covariance across a triggered vector, without temporal motion between draws. link::Classes/MultiOUProcess:: adds correlated innovations to mean-reverting trajectories. Several voices can share movement without being identical; the distinction is correlation across coordinates versus memory through time.

subsection:: Three meanings of regime

table::
## strong::Model:: || strong::What persists and what changes:: || strong::Audible use::
## link::Classes/RandomTelegraph:: || A discrete state itself persists for a random dwell, then switches || Alternate two persistent conditions
## link::Classes/MarkovModulatedTrig:: || A hidden Markov state changes event statistics; observed events do not cause the state change || Quiet and dense passages of the same sound
## link::Classes/DoubleWellProcess:: || A continuous nonlinear state spends time near preferred regions and crosses a barrier under noise || Metastable motion with visible transition paths
::

DoubleWellProcess can also demonstrate stochastic resonance: moderate noise can help a weak periodic drive organize barrier crossings. Increasing noise indefinitely does not improve that relationship. A discrete switch, a hidden intensity regime and continuous metastability are three different mechanisms, even when all produce alternating passages.

subsection:: Four meanings of irregular movement

table::
## strong::Model:: || strong::Mechanism and memory:: || strong::Practical consequence::
## link::Classes/OUProcess:: || Position plus a restoring drift, driven by Gaussian innovations || Local wandering around an equilibrium
## link::Classes/MaternNoise:: || Position and hidden derivatives with a prescribed stationary covariance || Change roughness while holding variance and correlation scale fixed
## link::Classes/LevyFlight:: || Accumulated independent stable increments, no restoring force || Local exploration with potentially very large relocations
## link::Classes/JumpDiffusion:: || Brownian wandering plus independent finite-rate Gaussian-marked Poisson shocks || Hear and accent shocks separately from continuous motion
::

OU and Matérn have finite stationary spread under fixed valid parameters. Lévy and jump diffusion accumulate displacement and have no stationary position distribution. For alpha below two the stable increment variance is infinite. Jump diffusion with finite Gaussian marks has finite increment moments and exposes jump count explicitly. These distinctions matter when selecting a register mapping or deciding whether a long performance should return toward a center.

subsection:: Four meanings of interaction

table::
## strong::Model:: || strong::What influences what:: || strong::Organization::
## link::Classes/MultiHawkesTrig:: || Events alter other streams' event intensities || Contagion and cascades in event time
## link::Classes/KuramotoNetwork:: || Phases respond to their population mean field || Collective phase synchronization
## link::Classes/WilsonCowan:: || Excitatory and inhibitory activities feed back on each other || Population rhythms, suppression and response
## link::Classes/Lorenz96:: || Neighboring spatial coordinates enter each local derivative || Propagating irregular structure around a ring
::

Interaction can organize timing, circular phase, population activity or spatial state. These are different feedback variables and topologies; replacing one with another changes the process rather than merely the timbre.

