subsection:: Raw state and explicit sonification

link::Classes/HenonSonify::, link::Classes/GbmanSonify:: and link::Classes/LatoocarfianSonify:: deliberately map coordinates into iteration frequency and level with their documented folding and interpolation. They are rendering strategies. The raw maps do not clip, fold, interpolate or interpret their coordinates as frequency. Similarly, a raw flow or transport process can diverge or produce extreme finite values; make audible range mapping explicit in the SynthDef. No general antialiasing guarantee follows from mathematical accuracy.

Seeded models use a local PCG32 generator. Nonzero seeds repeat their local algorithmic sequences for the same inputs and calculation rate; zero derives a seed from the parent Synth at construction. Floating library differences can affect last-bit values across platforms. Rising triggers, resets and reloads mean a nonpositive-to-positive edge. Round 3 reset wins over concurrent operations and consumes their edge histories. Check each page for whether reset rereads a buffer, restores a cached snapshot or only changes dynamic state.

subsection:: Buffers and calculation cost

Allocate/fill Buffers inside a Routine, call code::s.sync::, then create the Synth. Output dimensions and RK4 substeps are construction-time choices in Round 3. Snapshot buffers are private copies and need reload to see edits; older MarkovChain and SemiMarkov transition weights are live reads under the shared buffer lock. Reloading large matrices can cost more than an ordinary sample. Lorenz96's maximum cell/substep configuration is bounded but can exceed a core's audio-rate budget: use control rate when the musical role permits it.

subsection:: Choose a visualization that exposes the model

Use phase portraits for low-dimensional maps; a 3D orbit for Chua; delayed coordinates plus a time series for MackeyGlass; cell-index versus time heatmaps for CoupledMapLattice and Lorenz96. A ternary plot reveals Dirichlet allocation geometry. Probability histories and entropy reveal reinforcement. Event rasters with intensity/state traces expose hidden regimes. Covariance and increment plots distinguish Gaussian smoothness from heavy-tailed transport. Do not normalize away jumps or label a trajectory as a terrain unless a scalar field is actually being read.

link::Classes/SDPhaseView:: is a language-side display/sonification helper, not a UGen or stochastic model. It transports the exact shared server state only at display rate and does its bounded drawing/storage outside the audio thread. Class pages contain process-specific figures and minimal examples; the few combinations below teach relationships across models. Earlier runnable guide examples are retained.

subsection:: Distribution, process, and map

code::
(
{
    var trigger = Impulse.kr(8);
    var independent = TGammaRand.kr(2, 200, trigger);
    var continuous = OUProcess.kr(0, 2, 0.5);
    var map = HenonMap.kr(trigger);
    var freq = independent + map[0].linlin(-1.5, 1.5, 100, 1000);
    Pan2.ar(SinOsc.ar(freq, 0, 0.08), continuous.tanh)
}.play
)
::

subsection:: Fresh allocation versus accumulated preference

Dirichlet draws redistribute a whole independently. The urn gradually changes its own future choice probabilities. On the left all four voices share a newly drawn budget; on the right one register is chosen from accumulated history. Both begin from equal parameters, with no Markov transition matrix.

code::
(
Routine {
    var equal = Buffer.loadCollection(s, [1, 1, 1, 1]);
    var node;
    s.sync;
    node = {
        var trigger = Impulse.kr(5);
        var voices = SinOsc.ar([220, 330, 550, 880]);
        var shares = TDirichletRand.kr(4, equal, trigger, seed: 401);
        var choice = PolyaUrn.kr(4, equal, trigger, seed: 48)[0];
        [Mix(voices * Lag.kr(shares, 0.02)), Select.ar(choice, voices)]
            * Decay2.kr(trigger, 0.003, 0.12) * 0.07
    }.play;
    20.wait; node.free; s.sync; equal.free;
}.play;
)
::

subsection:: Self-exciting events

code::
(
{
    var event = HawkesTrig.ar(0.4, 18, 22)[0];
    var freq = TLogNormalRand.ar(6.5, 0.6, event).clip(100, 8000);
    var env = Decay2.ar(event, 0.001, 0.08);
    SinOsc.ar(freq, 0, env * 0.1)
}.play
)
::

subsection:: Interaction versus inhibition

The same sound makes clustered timing on the left and self-correcting spacing on the right, at comparable average densities.

code::
(
{
    var events = [HawkesTrig.ar(2, 8, 12)[0], SelfCorrectingTrig.ar(6, 1.5)[0]];
    SinOsc.ar(600, 0, Decay2.ar(events, 0.001, 0.05) * 0.08)
}.play
)
::

subsection:: Regimes bias a metastable system

A telegraph state changes which well is favored. The continuous process and its noise still determine whether and when a crossing occurs.

code::
(
{
    var bias = RandomTelegraph.kr(-0.25, 0.25, 0.4, 0.4)[0];
    var state = DoubleWellProcess.kr(bias, noise: 0.7, timeScale: 6, substeps: 4);
    Pan2.ar(RLPF.ar(Saw.ar(90, 0.06), state.tanh.linexp(-1, 1, 200, 4000), 0.2))
        * Line.kr(0, 1, 0.05)
}.play
)
::

subsection:: Related drift with circular event placement

Two related pitch trajectories move continuously, while transient positions are sampled on a four-channel ring around a shared preferred direction. Use a four-channel output setup for the full spatial example.

code::
(
Routine {
    var covariance = Buffer.loadCollection(s, [1, 0.75, 0.75, 1]);
    var node;
    s.sync;
    node = {
        var drift = MultiOUProcess.kr(2, covariance);
        var events = [Impulse.kr(4), Impulse.kr(3)];
        var angles = TVonMisesRand.kr(SinOsc.kr(0.03) * pi, 4, events);
        Mix.fill(2, { |i|
            PanAz.ar(4, SinOsc.ar([330, 495][i] * drift[i].midiratio, 0,
                Decay2.kr(events[i], 0.003, 0.15) * 0.06), angles[i] / pi)
        }) * Line.kr(0, 1, 0.05)
    }.play;
    20.wait;
    node.free;
    s.sync;
    covariance.free;
}.play;
)
::

subsection:: Cascades increase collective coordination

Event interaction changes oscillator interaction: a Hawkes cascade temporarily strengthens Kuramoto coupling. Quiet intervals let the narrow frequency spread loosen the phases again.

code::
(
Routine {
    var base = Buffer.loadCollection(s, [0.5, 0]);
    var excite = Buffer.loadCollection(s, [5, 2, 10, 3]);
    var decay = Buffer.loadCollection(s, [12, 12, 12, 12]);
    var frequencies = Buffer.loadCollection(s, Array.series(8, 0.8, 0.06));
    var node;
    s.sync;
    node = {
        var activity = MultiHawkesTrig.kr(2, base, excite, decay)[0..1].sum;
        var coupling = Decay.kr(activity, 1.5) * 8;
        var phases = KuramotoNetwork.kr(8, frequencies, coupling: coupling, substeps: 4)[0..7];
        var pulses = ((phases.cos + 1) * 0.5).pow(10);
        Splay.ar(SinOsc.ar(Array.geom(8, 200, 1.12), 0, pulses * 0.035))
            * Line.kr(0, 1, 0.05)
    }.play;
    25.wait;
    node.free;
    s.sync;
    [base, excite, decay, frequencies].do(_.free);
}.play;
)
::

subsection:: Microscopic material and slower form

ShotNoise provides the individual particles. Inhibitory event timing changes the resonant material at a slower scale, keeping texture and form distinct.

code::
(
{
    var change = SelfCorrectingTrig.kr(0.7, 2)[0];
    var root = Lag.kr(TChoose.kr(change, [180, 240, 320]), 0.05);
    var particles = ShotNoise.ar(35, 0.005, 0, 0.4)[0];
    Splay.ar(Ringz.ar(particles, root * [1, 1.7, 2.6], 0.2))
        * 0.015 * Line.kr(0, 1, 0.05)
}.play
)
::
